#N canvas 40 60 1280 1010 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 14\ \ \ Transformers\ \ -\ \ an\ induction\ head\ continues\ your\ melody\ (2017\ /\ 2022) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 980 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 980 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 980 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 How does a transformer continue a sequence it has never seen? Researchers looking inside trained transformers found INDUCTION HEADS: attention circuits that look back for the last time the current context appeared and copy what came next. Here is one \, wired by hand \, continuing a melody note by note., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 890 empty empty THE\ IDEA 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 A transformer reads a whole sequence and \, for the newest element \, ATTENDS to all earlier ones (lesson 13): query . key scores \, softmax with a temperature \, a weighted mix of values., f 52;
#X text 20 188 INDUCTION HEAD (Olsson et al. \, Anthropic \, 2022): the query is "what is happening now" (the current note and the one before) \, each key is "what was happening just before position j" \, and the value is the note AT position j. Attention finds earlier moments that look like now \, and the head predicts that the same thing will follow again., f 52;
#X text 20 314 That is IN-CONTEXT LEARNING in its simplest form: the network was not trained on your melody \, it copies patterns from the prompt itself. Large language models do this with words - the same circuit \, learned instead of hand-wired., f 52;
#X text 20 395 TEMPERATURE: low = always copy the best match (loops the motif) \, high = sometimes jump to other matches (variations) \, very high = random notes from the memory. RECENCY prefers recent matches. CONTEXT = how much the note before the current one matters (2 notes of context instead of 1)., f 52;
#X text 20 506 WATCH OUT for loops: when a repetition enters the memory it matches itself and can repeat forever - the same "degenerate repetition" large language models show. Raise the temperature to escape., f 52;
#X text 20 587 Music Transformer (Huang et al. \, 2018) and MusicGen (2023) are full transformers over notes and audio tokens - many heads \, many layers \, all learned., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 860 430 empty empty MEMORY\ (top)\ \ and\ \ ATTENTION\ (bottom) 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
#X obj 430 136 cnv 1 640 140 empty empty empty 4 10 0 12 #ffffff #000000 0;
#N canvas 0 50 450 250 (subpatch) 0;
#X array seqv\$0 32 float 10;
#X coords 0 84 32 48 640 140 1 0 0;
#X restore 430 136 graph;
#X obj 430 300 cnv 1 640 110 empty empty empty 4 10 0 12 #ffffff #000000 0;
#N canvas 0 50 450 250 (subpatch) 0;
#X array att\$0 32 float 10;
#X coords 0 1 32 0 640 110 1 0 0;
#X restore 430 300 graph;
#X text 1084 136 TOP: the notes in memory (MIDI 48 .. 84) \, oldest left \, newest right., f 24;
#X text 1084 300 BOTTOM: the attention weights of the last prediction. A tall bar under a note = "copy THIS note next"., f 24;
#X text 430 420 Generate with a low temperature and watch the bars: they sit under the notes that followed earlier occurrences of the current context., f 90;
